# Overview
#
# An adverb is a word that describes a verb, an adjective, or a whole
# sentence, such as `safely` or `also`. A sentence adverb comments on
# the whole sentence after it instead of on one verb or adjective. Each
# adverb in this rule opens a sentence by rating the point that follows
# (`Clearly, the cache is cold`, `Unfortunately, the cache is cold`).
# The rating tells the reader how to judge the point and adds no fact to
# it. The fix deletes the adverb and lets the point carry its own
# weight. The rule sits at Required, because each adverb counts only
# with a comma glued to it and the fix is mechanical.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` matches each adverb only with a comma glued to it, the form
# that a sentence adverb takes. `obviously` and `clearly` also need a
# word after the comma that can open a new clause. A clause is a subject
# and its verb, such as `the tests pass`. The other adverbs rarely end a
# clause, so they count with the comma alone.
#
# Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks each word with a tag for its word
# class, and a pattern names a tag in capitals. The word after the comma
# carries the tag for a determiner (`DET`), a pronoun (`PRON`), a proper
# noun (`PROPN`), or a noun (`NOUN`). A determiner is a word that
# introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`. A proper noun is a
# name that takes a capital letter, such as `Kubernetes`. By default, a
# Weir match stops at a comma, so `let scope "Sentence"` lets the
# `obviously` and `clearly` branch cross one.
#
# False Positives
#
# The bare form of each adverb describes a verb or an adjective (`the
# error prints clearly`, `obviously irrelevant`), so it passes.
#
# `clearly` and `obviously` can also end a clause before a comma as
# manner adverbs, which say how something happens (`to see it more
# clearly, consider the listing`). The word that the pattern needs after
# the comma keeps that sense out.
#
# `Ideally,` stays out, because it marks a goal that the system can miss
# (`Ideally, every controller fits the specification`). Deleting it
# turns the goal into a claim.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseRestatement reports `to be clear,` and guards it the
# same way, with a word after the comma that can open a new clause.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 58 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits were sentence adverbs.
expr main [([obviously, clearly], [DET, PRON, PROPN, NOUN]), (interestingly,), (importantly,), (notably,), (crucially,), (unfortunately,), (fortunately,), (luckily,), (thankfully,), (surprisingly,), (unsurprisingly,), (sadly,), (remarkably,), (admittedly,)]
let message "Sentence adverb. Delete it and let the point carry its own weight."
let description "Flags a sentence-opening adverb such as `clearly`, `notably`, or `unfortunately` that rates the point that follows."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The error prints clearly on a dark background."
allows "Do not make the negative queries obviously irrelevant."
allows "To see it more clearly, consider the listing below."
allows "The log shows the cause clearly, so start there."
allows "Ideally, every controller fits the specification."
